nted D8. Black's lead fell from -2.4 to -13.8, and Opus 5.5 was suddenly far ahead."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "Opus 5.5 was well ahead at -17.9 for Black, then played D9 and lost 11.7 points, taking Black's lead to -6.3. The engine preferred F4, and Opus 5.5 played F4 two moves later at no cost. A large part of the early advantage was gone by this point."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "After White's H7 pushed the lead to +8.8 for Black, the 12k replied with H6, which lost 16.7 points and was the worst move of the game. The engine's choice was G7, and Black's lead dropped to -7.8."}, {"turn": 38, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with H4 and lost 15.9 points, its worst move of the game. The engine wanted G7, and Black's lead jumped from -7.8 to +8.1, undoing the 12k's mistake on the previous turn."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "The 12k's J5 lost 6.1 points, then Opus 5.5 played H9 and lost 7.7, with the engine preferring D2. Black's lead went from -6.0 to +1.8. From here it stayed between +1.8 and +2.2 until the final pass."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py applies one named move and reports captures and group liberties, libs.py prints group liberties, and count.py counts stones and territory on a given board. None of them enumerate candidate moves, score points, or play out continuations, so they only inspect the current position and the effect of a move the player chose.", "stop": false}}